  <h2 class="help-group-h">Getting started</h2>

  <!-- 1. Quick Start (open by default) -->
  <div id="hacc-quickstart" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-quickstart')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2 4.5 13.5H11l-1 8.5 8.5-11.5H12z"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Quick Start — pick your path</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Five paths · a few minutes each</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <p class="help-p">Paths A to D produce a ready-to-run VuGen script ZIP in a few minutes; path E turns the results of a test run into a report. Choose the one that matches your starting point.</p>
        <div class="help-paths">
          <div class="help-path" style="--c-tool:var(--c-convert)">
            <div class="help-path-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2 2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h1M16 3h1a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2 2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2h-1"/></svg>Path A — Postman / Bruno</div>
            <ol class="help-path-steps">
              <li>Click <strong>Postman / Bruno</strong> in the sidebar</li>
              <li>Upload your .json / .yml / .bru file</li>
              <li>Choose output format → <strong>Convert</strong></li>
              <li>Download ZIP → open in VuGen</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="help-path" style="--c-tool:var(--c-jmeter)">
            <div class="help-path-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M17 3l4 4-4 4"/><path d="M3 7h18"/><path d="M7 21l-4-4 4-4"/><path d="M21 17H3"/></svg>Path B — JMeter (.jmx)</div>
            <ol class="help-path-steps">
              <li>Click <strong>JMeter</strong> in the sidebar</li>
              <li>Upload your .jmx file + any CSV files</li>
              <li>Choose output format → <strong>Convert</strong></li>
              <li>Download ZIP → open in VuGen</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="help-path" style="--c-tool:var(--c-record)">
            <div class="help-path-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3.5" fill="currentColor"/></svg>Path C — Record in browser</div>
            <ol class="help-path-steps">
              <li>Click <strong>Script Studio</strong> in the sidebar</li>
              <li>Once per computer: get the CDP Recorder, extract it and double-click <code>cdp-recorder.exe</code></li>
              <li>Click <strong>Start recording</strong>, type your application's address, and use it in the recording window — naming your transactions</li>
              <li><strong>Stop and save</strong> — the recording opens in Script Studio by itself, the script ready (or drop a <code>.har</code> file exported from DevTools)</li>
              <li>Check the requests → <strong>Download ZIP</strong></li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="help-path" style="--c-tool:var(--c-studio)">
            <div class="help-path-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg>Path D — Deep correlation</div>
            <ol class="help-path-steps">
              <li>Click <strong>Script Studio</strong> in the sidebar</li>
              <li>Open a recording → step 2, <strong>Correlate</strong> (for two recordings: <strong>Advanced: second recording</strong>)</li>
              <li>Tick the values in the table → <strong>Add n to the script</strong></li>
              <li>A value still missing? <strong>HAR Explorer</strong> → <strong>Find a value</strong> → <strong>Add to Script Studio</strong></li>
              <li>Download ZIP → open in VuGen</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="help-path" style="--c-tool:var(--c-results)">
            <div class="help-path-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 3v18h18"/><path d="M7 15v-4M11 15V8M15 15v-6M19 15V5"/></svg>Path E — Results of a test run</div>
            <ol class="help-path-steps">
              <li>Click <strong>Results Analyser</strong> in the sidebar</li>
              <li>Choose the run's <strong>raw results</strong> ZIP from LRE (or the unzipped folder)</li>
              <li>Review Overview, Transactions, Charts, Errors and Workload</li>
              <li>Export <strong>Excel</strong>, <strong>Word</strong>, <strong>HTML</strong> or <strong>PDF</strong></li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 2. What is PerfX Studio? -->
  <div id="hacc-overview" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-overview')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M12 16v-5M12 8h.01"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">What is PerfX Studio?</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Seven tools · any input → VuGen script → workload → results report</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <p class="help-p">PerfX Studio turns any performance testing input — Postman/Bruno collections, JMeter test plans, or browser recordings — into production-ready VuGen scripts in minutes. It handles the work that normally takes days: correlation, parameterisation, authentication, and transaction boundaries. Three tools create scripts: the two converters and <strong>Script Studio</strong> (record or open a recording, correlate, download). The <strong>HAR Explorer</strong> shows what is inside a recording and where each dynamic value comes from. The <strong>Results Analyser</strong> turns the raw results of a LoadRunner Enterprise run into a clear report. The small <strong>CDP Recorder</strong> program records your journey in Edge or Chrome for Script Studio.</p>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Tool</th>
              <th>What you give it</th>
              <th>What you get</th>
              <th>Best for</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-convert)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2 2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h1M16 3h1a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2 2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2h-1"/></svg>Postman / Bruno Converter</td><td>.json / .yml / .bru collection</td><td>VuGen script ZIP</td><td>Teams already using Postman or Bruno</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-jmeter)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M17 3l4 4-4 4"/><path d="M3 7h18"/><path d="M7 21l-4-4 4-4"/><path d="M21 17H3"/></svg>JMeter Converter</td><td>.jmx file + optional CSVs</td><td>VuGen script ZIP + Workload Excel</td><td>Migrating from JMeter to LoadRunner</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-studio)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg>Script Studio</td><td>A recording made with <strong>Start recording</strong> (CDP Recorder), or 1 or 2 HAR files from DevTools</td><td>VuGen script ZIP, correlated when needed, with test data</td><td>Recording a new journey &#x2014; also on virtual desktops and Azure machines where VuGen can&#x27;t record &#x2014; and any app with tokens, session IDs, or CSRF values</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-explore)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="7"/><path d="m20 20-3.5-3.5"/><path d="M8 11h6M11 8v6"/></svg>HAR Explorer</td><td>Browser HAR file</td><td>Every request, where each dynamic value comes from, and the correlation for Script Studio</td><td>A value that breaks a replay, or one the Correlation Advisor missed &#x2014; also where DevTools is switched off</td></tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-work)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 19V5M4 19h16"/><path d="m7 15 4-4 3 3 5-6"/></svg>Workload Modeller</td>
              <td>Script ZIPs or HAR files, and each script's target</td>
              <td>LRE settings to copy and a one-sheet Excel file for the scenario</td>
              <td>Working out Vusers, pacing and load generators before a test</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="help-tool-name" style="--c-tool:var(--c-results)"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 3v18h18"/><path d="M7 15v-4M11 15V8M15 15v-6M19 15V5"/></svg>Results Analyser</td>
              <td>LRE raw results folder or ZIP</td>
              <td>Excel, Word, PDF and HTML report</td>
              <td>Understanding a test run and sharing the results</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-blue">
          <strong><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg>Privacy first.</strong> Script Studio, the HAR Explorer and the Results Analyser work in your browser — recordings and results are not uploaded. The two converters upload your files to the server: while a conversion runs they are in its memory and its temporary folder, and they are deleted as soon as the conversion ends. Generated scripts stay in memory; nothing is kept between requests. Download links expire after 5 minutes and are single-use.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="help-group-h">Tools</h2>

  <!-- 4. Postman / Bruno Converter -->
  <div id="hacc-converter" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-convert)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-converter')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-convert)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2 2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h1M16 3h1a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2 2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2h-1"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Postman / Bruno Converter</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Upload a collection → get a VuGen script ZIP</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-blue"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2 2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h1M16 3h1a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v4a2 2 0 0 0 2 2 2 2 0 0 0-2 2v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2h-1"/></svg>Postman / Bruno Converter</div>
        <p class="help-p">Convert your API collection directly into a VuGen script — no scripting knowledge required. The converter reads all requests, headers, bodies, auth, and pre/post scripts, and generates a complete VuGen project.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Supported input formats</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>Postman v2.1</strong> — export from Postman: <em>Collection → ⋯ → Export → Collection v2.1</em></li>
          <li><strong>Bruno JSON</strong> — the <code>.json</code> export from Bruno desktop</li>
          <li><strong>Bruno YAML</strong> — <code>.yml</code> or <code>.yaml</code> collection files</li>
          <li><strong>Bruno folder</strong> — a zipped directory of <code>.bru</code> files</li>
          <li><strong>Single .bru file</strong> — a single Bruno request</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="help-p">Optionally upload an <strong>Environment file</strong> alongside your collection. It fills in variable values — base URLs, API keys, usernames — directly in the generated script so you do not need to edit them by hand.</p>
        <p class="help-p">After <strong>Convert</strong>, one screen shows the script. <strong>Every choice changes it at once</strong> &#x2014; there is nothing to apply, and <strong>Download ZIP</strong> always gives what you see. At the top: the script&#x2019;s name (click it to rename; it names the .usr file and the ZIP), <strong>Files</strong> (change, add or remove files, or convert another), <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Redo</strong> (Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y), <strong>Plan the load</strong> (opens the script in the Workload Modeller) and Download; the number beside it opens <strong>Ready to download?</strong>, a list of things worth a look. Below: <strong>Format</strong> (Web HTTP/HTML or DevWeb), <strong>Scripts</strong> (one, or one per folder), <strong>Think time</strong> and <strong>Settings</strong> (log level; correlation, parameterization, authentication and comments on or off). Your settings are remembered.</p>
        <p class="help-p"><strong>Domains</strong> lists every server the requests call, with its parameter in the script. Click the parameter to change its name &#x2014; a variable of your environment or collection (<code>base_url</code>) is renamed everywhere it is used &#x2014; or its address. Click a domain to see only its requests; untick it to leave them out. <em>Needs a value</em> marks one that is still a variable with no value.</p>
        <p class="help-p"><strong>Requests</strong> are grouped by folder, in the order of the collection, which is the order in the script. Untick a request, a folder or a domain to leave it out; left-out ones stay listed, struck through, and <strong>Put back</strong> returns them. If a request you keep sends a value that only a left-out request sets &#x2014; a token, an id &#x2014; a note says so. Click a request to see its code in <strong>Script</strong>; the <strong>Request</strong> tab shows its headers, body, scripts and values (secrets written out are hidden).</p>
        <p class="help-p"><strong>Values</strong> lists every value the requests send, in groups you can fold: the ones that <strong>need a value</strong> first (type it there &#x2014; your files are not changed; an empty path variable such as <code>/orders/:id</code> is one of them), then those set while the script runs (read from a response, or made by a script of the collection), from the environment, from the collection, from a data file, and made by the script. The pencil changes a value; <em>n uses</em> shows the requests that send it. If an update does not work (server busy, no connection), the last script and all your choices stay, with <strong>Try again</strong>. Hide a panel with the button in its header, drag the bar before Script to resize it (or use its arrow keys; double-click puts it back) &#x2014; remembered in this browser.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Step-by-step</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>Click <strong>Postman / Bruno</strong> in the sidebar.</li>
          <li>Drop your collection on the page, or click <strong>Choose file</strong>. Drop the environment file and any client certificates too (optional) &#x2014; each goes to its box.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Convert</strong>.</li>
          <li>Choose the <strong>Format</strong>: <strong>DevWeb</strong> (JavaScript) or <strong>Web HTTP/HTML</strong> (C). The script changes at once.</li>
          <li>If <strong>Values</strong> says some need a value, type them there. Leave out what you do not need, rename what you like.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Download ZIP</strong>.</li>
          <li>Extract the ZIP and open the <code>.usr</code> file in VuGen, or upload it to LoadRunner Enterprise.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-blue">
          <strong>Multi-script:</strong> Choose this under Scripts when your collection has multiple folders. Instead of one large script, you get one separate script per folder — ideal for modular test design.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. JMeter Converter -->
  <div id="hacc-jmeter" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-jmeter)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-jmeter')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-jmeter)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M17 3l4 4-4 4"/><path d="M3 7h18"/><path d="M7 21l-4-4 4-4"/><path d="M21 17H3"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">JMeter Converter</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Migrate .jmx test plans to VuGen + Workload Excel</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-orange"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M17 3l4 4-4 4"/><path d="M3 7h18"/><path d="M7 21l-4-4 4-4"/><path d="M21 17H3"/></svg>JMeter Converter</div>
        <p class="help-p">Migrating from Apache JMeter to LoadRunner? Upload your <code>.jmx</code> test plan and receive an equivalent VuGen script, plus a <strong>Workload Model Excel</strong> file that translates your JMeter thread groups into a LoadRunner workload definition.</p>
        <p class="help-p">After <strong>Convert</strong>, the same screen as for Postman / Bruno shows the script, and every choice changes it at once. The requests are grouped by thread group, with its users, ramp-up, hold time and loops; a thread group with every request left out gets no script. <strong>Values</strong> shows where each value comes from &#x2014; the test plan, a CSV data set, an extractor &#x2014; and lets you give one a value. A CSV file the plan reads and that was not added is named at the top, with a button to add it; an added one lies next to every script that reads it. <strong>Settings</strong> includes or leaves out the workload workbook. <strong>Plan the load</strong> opens the scripts in the Workload Modeller.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">What to upload</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>.jmx file</strong> — your JMeter test plan (exported from JMeter: <em>File → Save Test Plan As</em>)</li>
          <li><strong>CSV files</strong> — any data files referenced by CSVDataSet elements in the plan (optional but recommended)</li>
          <li><strong>Certificate files</strong> — client certificates (.pem / .p12) used for mTLS authentication (optional)</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Step-by-step</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>Click <strong>JMeter</strong> in the sidebar.</li>
          <li>Drop your <code>.jmx</code> file on the page, with the CSV and certificate files it uses, or click <strong>Choose file</strong>.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Convert</strong>.</li>
          <li>Choose the <strong>Format</strong> (<strong>DevWeb</strong> or <strong>Web HTTP/HTML</strong>) and <strong>Scripts</strong>: <strong>Single</strong> or <strong>Per thread group</strong>.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Download ZIP</strong> — it includes VuGen scripts and a Workload Model Excel file.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-amber">
          <strong>Per-thread-group mode:</strong> Each JMeter Thread Group becomes its own VuGen script. Recommended when thread groups represent different user roles or business flows.
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">What gets converted</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li>HTTP Sampler requests → VuGen web requests with headers and bodies</li>
          <li>CSVDataSet → VuGen parameterisation with matching data files</li>
          <li>Extractors (JSON, RegEx, XPath, Boundary) → equivalent VuGen correlation code</li>
          <li>setUp / tearDown Thread Groups → <code>vuser_init.c</code> / <code>vuser_end.c</code></li>
          <li>JSR223 Groovy scripts → converted where possible, flagged where manual review needed</li>
          <li>Thread Group settings → Workload Model Excel (ramp, users, duration)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 6. Script Studio (record, review, correlate, download — the Recorder is part of it) -->
  <div id="hacc-studio" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-studio)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-studio')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-studio)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Script Studio</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Record in Edge or Chrome, or open a HAR → VuGen script · correlation, test data · works when VuGen recording is blocked</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-purple"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"/></svg>Script Studio</div>
        <p class="help-p">Script Studio takes you from a recording to a VuGen script in one workspace: record your journey (or open a HAR file), review the requests, correlate the dynamic values, set the test data and download the ZIP. The script is ready as soon as the recording opens &#x2014; a plain script needs nothing more. The workspace has four steps, shown at the top: <strong>1 Requests</strong> (domains, requests, transactions and the script), <strong>2 Correlate</strong> (the Correlation Advisor), <strong>3 Test data</strong> and <strong>4 Download</strong> (what is worth a look beside the script files with their code, then the ZIP; <strong>Plan the load</strong> opens the script in the Workload Modeller, also from the file bar). Click a step, or <strong>Next</strong> / <strong>Back</strong>; no step blocks another, and <strong>Download ZIP</strong> is always at the top.</p>
        <p class="help-p">Above the filters on step 1: <strong>Script name</strong> (letters, numbers and underscores, starting with a letter; the ZIP and the <code>.usr</code> file take it; empty = the default name; a new recording starts empty) and <strong>Think time between transactions</strong>: <strong>Fixed</strong> (3 seconds unless you change it, as before), <strong>As recorded</strong> (the pause in the recording between one transaction and the next, in whole seconds; a pause under a second writes nothing) or <strong>None</strong>. Never after the last transaction; your choice is remembered. In LoadRunner the run-time settings decide how the think time is replayed (as recorded, multiplied, random, limited or ignored).</p>
        <p class="help-p">In a standard workflow, VuGen records user journeys directly via its built-in proxy. However, on some corporate virtual desktops and Azure virtual machines, VuGen recording does not function due to security policies and incomplete installations. Script Studio addresses this: the <strong>CDP Recorder</strong> — a small Windows program, no administrator rights needed — records your journey in Edge or Chrome as a HAR file (or you export one from the browser's Developer Tools, F12), and Script Studio turns it into a structured VuGen script. (Script Studio includes what was the Recorder tool; old Recorder links open Script Studio.)</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">What is a HAR file?</h3>
        <p class="help-p">A HAR (HTTP Archive) file is a recording of all the network requests your browser made during a session. Every modern browser's Developer Tools (F12) can export one. It contains all the URLs, request headers, request bodies, and responses for everything that happened during your recording.</p>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-green">
          <strong>Three ways to record — choose what works for you:</strong><br><br>
          <strong>Option A — Start recording in Script Studio (recommended):</strong> once <code>cdp-recorder.exe</code> has been double-clicked one time on your computer, click <strong>Start recording</strong> on Script Studio&#x2019;s start screen — type your application's address, choose Edge or Chrome and, if you like, a private window. The recording window opens on your application, already recording; the page shows every request as it happens; <strong>Stop and save</strong> saves the HAR to your Downloads folder and opens it in Script Studio, with the script ready. See <em>Step-by-step (Start recording)</em> below.
          <a href="<%= basePath %>/downloads/cdp-recorder">Get CDP Recorder →</a><br><br>
          <strong>Option B — the CDP Recorder on its own:</strong> double-click <code>cdp-recorder.exe</code>, click <strong>Start Recording</strong> on its floating toolbar, record your journey (new tabs and pop-ups too), name transactions there, and click <strong>Stop Recording</strong>. The HAR is saved next to <code>cdp-recorder.exe</code> — drop it on Script Studio.<br><br>
          <strong>Option C — Bookmarks + DevTools:</strong> nothing to install. Drag the two transaction buttons from Script Studio&#x2019;s <em>How to record your journey</em> to your bookmarks bar, open DevTools (F12), record your journey, export the HAR, then drop it here.
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Step-by-step (Start recording — CDP Recorder)</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li><strong>Once per computer:</strong> download <code>cdp-recorder.zip</code> from Script Studio (<em>How to record your journey</em>), right-click it → <strong>Extract All…</strong> to a folder that stays and where your computer lets you run programs (for example <code>C:\PerfX\cdp-recorder</code> — not inside your user profile folder (such as Downloads, Desktop or Documents); if Windows blocks the program there, ask your IT team), and double-click <code>cdp-recorder.exe</code> once. That connects it to the <strong>Start recording</strong> button for your Windows user — no administrator rights needed. Extracting alone is not enough. If you move the folder, double-click it again.</li>
          <li>Open <strong>Script Studio</strong> in the sidebar and click <strong>Start recording</strong>.</li>
          <li>Type your application's address, choose <strong>Edge</strong> or <strong>Chrome</strong>, and choose whether to <strong>Open a private window</strong> (InPrivate / Incognito) — optional: untick it if your application does not work in a private window. Either way the recording window starts signed out, in a fresh browser profile, so the login is recorded too. <em>More options</em>: a recording name (used for the file name), a <strong>script ID</strong> (optional, for example SC01: the file and the transactions in the script start with it, SC01_01_&#x2026;), a first transaction that starts when the page opens (numbered for you: <em>Launch</em> becomes <code>T01_Launch</code>), and — optional — your planned steps, one per line. Click <strong>Start recording</strong>.</li>
          <li>The first time the CDP Recorder is not running, your browser asks <em>"Open cdp-recorder?"</em> — click <strong>Open</strong>. It then waits in the background — no window (CDP Recorder 0.3.2) — and closes itself once a saved recording&#x2019;s windows have closed; Start recording starts it again (an older one still running is replaced by the newer). If nothing answers, the window shows the set-up steps.</li>
          <li>If your computer does not let the browser start programs, start <code>cdp-recorder.exe</code> yourself first, then click <strong>Start recording</strong>: the small CDP Recorder toolbar asks <em>"Allow this page to start recordings?"</em> — click <strong>Allow</strong> and the recording starts by itself. It asks once per PerfX Studio address and remembers it. If Windows does not allow <code>cdp-recorder.exe</code> to run at all, ask your IT team to approve it.</li>
          <li>An orange-bordered <strong>recording window</strong> opens maximised on your application, already recording, with the floating bar at the top of its pages (with the bar turned off, the small separate <strong>toolbar</strong> window opens beside it instead, as with VuGen). Use your application <strong>only in the recording window</strong> — new tabs and pop-ups it opens are recorded too; your usual browser is not.</li>
          <li><strong>The floating bar</strong> (CDP Recorder 0.3.0): a small dark bar at the top of the recording window (which opens maximised) shows REC, the time and the running transaction, with the same box: type a name and press <strong>Enter</strong> (or <strong>Mark</strong>) and the running transaction ends and the next starts &#x2014; there is no End on the bar. <strong>&#x25BE;</strong> opens the details: every transaction with its requests, rename (pencil), <strong>Undo last mark</strong>, the planned steps. <strong>Stop and save</strong> is on the bar too. Drag it anywhere in the window — by its grip or any part that is not a button — it stays where you let it go, remembered (a double-click on the grip puts it back at the top) — minimise it to a REC dot, or press <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd> to hide it and bring it back. One bar on screen: a new tab, window or pop-up takes the bar as it opens, and it follows you &#x2014; it shows in the recorded window you last clicked or typed in (and goes back when a pop-up closes). It stays above the application&#x2019;s own pop-ups, slims down in small windows, and is never part of the recording (no requests of its own, hidden in the transaction screenshots). Turn it off under <em>More options</em> &#x2192; <em>Floating bar in the recording window</em>.</li>
          <li>Name transactions on the page: type a name and press <strong>Enter</strong> — the running transaction ends and the new one starts. The number is added for you (<em>add to cart</em> becomes <code>T04_Add_to_cart</code>); type your own, for example <code>T07_Pay</code>, to use another number, and the next one follows it. With planned steps, press <strong>Enter</strong> on an empty box to start the next one — the list shows what is done and what is next. There is no End: the next name ends the running transaction, and <strong>Stop and save</strong> ends the last one. The title of the page on screen is offered as grey text in the box &#x2014; <strong>Tab</strong> takes it (never filled in by itself). A transaction&#x2019;s name can be changed in the list (the pencil); <strong>Undo last mark</strong> takes back the last mark &#x2014; the transaction before runs again. On the toolbar: type a name, <strong>Start Transaction</strong>, do the steps, then type the next name — starting the next transaction ends the open one, and so does Stop.</li>
          <li>The page shows the requests as they happen, grouped by transaction — the current one open, earlier ones folded; images, style sheets, scripts and fonts, and background calls, folded into one line each; requests from pop-ups and new tabs marked <em>window 2, window 3</em>; a find box; the list follows the newest request until you scroll up.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Stop and save</strong> (or Stop Recording on the toolbar). The HAR is saved to your <strong>Downloads</strong> folder as <code>&lt;name&gt;_&lt;date&gt;_&lt;time&gt;.har</code>, the recording windows close, and Script Studio opens the file with the script ready, in the format you chose &#x2014; as with a dropped file. The windows close after a 10-second countdown, shown on this page (or on the toolbar when the floating bar is off) — click <strong>Keep open</strong> to look at the recording window first, or <strong>Close windows</strong> to close them now. Quit on the toolbar during a recording saves it first, too.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="help-h3">How Start recording finds the CDP Recorder</h3>
        <p class="help-p">The first double-click of <code>cdp-recorder.exe</code> registers a <code>perfx-recorder:</code> link with Windows, for your user only (<code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Classes\perfx-recorder</code>, pointing to the <code>.exe</code> in your folder). <strong>Start recording</strong> opens that link, Windows starts the program, and the program talks to this page on your computer only (<code>127.0.0.1:8787</code>). Nothing is kept in the browser, so clearing history and cookies changes nothing; after moving the folder, double-click the <code>.exe</code> once more. Recordings run in a fresh, temporary browser profile, deleted when the recorder closes. More in the FAQ: <em>Where does the CDP Recorder keep its setup?</em></p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Step-by-step (Bookmarks + DevTools, or a HAR file you already have)</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>Open <strong>Script Studio</strong> in the sidebar, open <em>How to record your journey</em> → <strong>Bookmarks + DevTools</strong>, and drag the <strong>START Transaction</strong> and <strong>END Transaction</strong> buttons to your bookmarks bar. You only need to do this once.</li>
          <li>Open your browser and navigate to the application you want to test.</li>
          <li>Open <strong>DevTools</strong> (press F12) and go to the <strong>Network</strong> tab. Tick <strong>Preserve log</strong> and <strong>Disable cache</strong>, and make sure recording is on (the red circle should be active).</li>
          <li>Perform your complete user journey — log in, navigate pages, submit forms — exactly as a real user would. Click <strong>START Transaction</strong> before each transaction (type its name) and <strong>END Transaction</strong> after it.</li>
          <li>When done, in DevTools Network tab click the <strong>Export HAR</strong> button (the download icon) and save the <code>.har</code> file.</li>
          <li>Come back to Script Studio and drop the <code>.har</code> file anywhere on the page, or click <strong>Browse file</strong>. The script is made at once in the format you chose &#x2014; <strong>Web HTTP/HTML</strong> or <strong>DevWeb</strong> (remembered; switch it at any time in the file bar). (A recording made with Start recording arrives at this point by itself.)</li>
          <li>Use the <strong>Domains</strong> panel to switch off domains you do not want in your script (e.g. analytics, CDNs, fonts). If the script seems to need a domain &#x2014; a page of your journey, sign-in, a redirect to it, cookies &#x2014; Script Studio asks before leaving it out. The panel has three groups &#x2014; <strong>In the script</strong>, <strong>Not in the script</strong>, and <strong>Analytics / ads: always left out</strong> &#x2014; each folds.</li>
          <li>Use the type buttons in the filter bar (<strong>Doc</strong>, <strong>Fetch/XHR</strong>, <strong>JS</strong>, <strong>CSS</strong>, <strong>Img</strong>, <strong>Font</strong>, <strong>Media</strong>, <strong>Other</strong>) to choose which kinds of request are in the script; each shows how many requests it covers. <strong>Leave out</strong> takes out analytics / ads and OPTIONS requests. Requests left out are hidden: each transaction ends with one line saying how many and why &#x2014; click it to see them, or <strong>Show left-out</strong> for all of them. Always left out: browser reports, WebSocket, and a page the browser <strong>prefetched</strong> and then opened (the page itself stays &#x2014; it would otherwise load twice).</li>
          <li>To group requests into a new <strong>transaction</strong>, tick the requests in the <strong>Requests</strong> list and click <strong>New transaction</strong>. Transactions can be renamed or ungrouped, with Undo.</li>
          <li>Click a request to see its <strong>Headers</strong>, <strong>Payload</strong>, <strong>Response</strong>, <strong>Preview</strong>, <strong>Cookies</strong>, <strong>Timing</strong> and <strong>Dynamic values</strong>; the arrow keys move to the previous / next request and Esc (or a click outside) closes it. <strong>Hide secrets</strong> masks passwords, tokens and cookies for screenshots. <strong>Leave out</strong> takes this one request out of the script; <strong>Put back</strong> brings it back.</li>
          <li>The <strong>Script</strong> tab updates as you go.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Download ZIP</strong> and open it in VuGen &#x2014; or correlate first in step 2, <strong>Correlate</strong>.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="help-h3">What is correlation and why does it matter?</h3>
        <p class="help-p">When you log in to a web application, the server sends back a token or session ID. Every request after that must include this token. If your script has the token hard-coded from your recording session, it will fail when run again because the server issues a new token each time. Correlation solves this by extracting the token dynamically at runtime and passing it forward automatically.</p>
        <p class="help-p">Without correlation, your script will fail on replay. Script Studio generates the extraction and substitution code for you automatically.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">1 HAR vs 2 HARs</h3>
        <div class="help-formats">
          <div class="help-format-card help-format-purple1">
            <div class="help-format-title">1 HAR file</div>
            <p class="help-format-desc">Pattern-based analysis to find values that look dynamic (UUIDs, tokens, long random strings, timestamps). Fast and works well for simple applications.</p>
            <p class="help-format-rec"><strong>Use when:</strong> you have one recording and want a quick result. Some correlations may need manual adjustment.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="help-format-card help-format-purple2">
            <div class="help-format-title">2 HAR files (recommended)</div>
            <p class="help-format-desc">Record the exact same user journey twice with different data (e.g. two different logins). Script Studio compares both and finds every value that changed — guaranteed to be dynamic. Far more complete and accurate correlation.</p>
            <p class="help-format-rec"><strong>Use when:</strong> you want the most reliable script. Recommended for any app with login, tokens, or session management.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Step-by-step (correlation)</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>Open the recording in Script Studio (above). The <strong>Correlation Advisor</strong> has already checked it: step 2, <strong>Correlate</strong>, says how many values it found.</li>
          <li>For 2-HAR mode: record the same journey twice using different test credentials or data. In step 2, <strong>Correlate</strong>, open <strong>Advanced: second recording</strong> and drop the second HAR file there &#x2014; or <strong>Record it now</strong>: it loads as the second recording (diff mode).</li>
          <li>Tick the values to correlate in the table (High ones come ticked; <strong>Review</strong> opens one in the editor), then click <strong>Add n to the script</strong>. A value the Advisor did not list? <strong>Can&#x2019;t find a value?</strong> &#x2192; <strong>Find a value</strong> (below), or open the same file in the <strong>HAR Explorer</strong>, <strong>Find a value</strong>, and <strong>Add to Script Studio</strong> — it comes back here marked <em>From HAR Explorer</em>.</li>
          <li>Set the test data in step 3, <strong>Test data</strong> (below).</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Download ZIP</strong> and open the script in VuGen.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Correlation Advisor</h3>
        <p class="help-p"><strong>Step 2, Correlate</strong> shows what the Correlation Advisor found as one table: a tick, the <strong>Name</strong> (change it there), <strong>What it is</strong>, the <strong>Value</strong>, where it <strong>Comes from</strong> and where it is <strong>Sent back in</strong> (click a request to open it; <strong>+n more</strong> lists every place), and the <strong>Confidence</strong> (High: an earlier response sends the value; Medium: found by its shape only). High values come ticked when you open the step. Filter by text, type and confidence; show <strong>To review</strong>, <strong>In the script</strong>, <strong>Skipped</strong> or <strong>All</strong>; <strong>Select all high</strong>, <strong>Clear selection</strong>, <strong>Skip</strong> / <strong>Put back</strong>. Nothing changes in the script until you click <strong>Add n to the script</strong>; leaving the step with ticked values asks first (<strong>Not now</strong> keeps them ticked). <strong>Add manual correlation</strong> lets you pick a value yourself, in the same editor: choose the request on the left (by default only those whose response has a value a later request sends back), then click one of the marked values in its response on the right, or select any text &#x2014; how it is read, its name, the code and where it is sent back fill in, and <strong>Add to the script</strong> puts in that value only. The Correlation Advisor suggests a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> whose options look generated (flights, slots) by itself when a later request sends one of them back (<strong>Options of a select</strong> in the table). A value that is one of many (the options of a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code>, the items of a JSON list) can also be read as a <strong>List</strong>: choose a value and click <strong>List</strong> (or the link the test shows), or select the whole section (a whole <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> works however its options are written, <code>selected</code> first or not). A list asks which value is sent back: <strong>Every value, as recorded</strong>, <strong>One at random each iteration</strong>, <strong>The next one each iteration</strong> or <strong>Always the same one</strong> (a position) &#x2014; the same choice is offered for the lists the Correlation Advisor finds. To add several, click <strong>Keep, pick another</strong> after each one: the kept values are listed under <strong>Values to add</strong> (marked green in the response) and <strong>Add all n to the script</strong> puts them all in; <strong>Can&#x2019;t find a value?</strong> leads to Find a value, Paste &amp; Detect (an empty response) and the second recording. <strong>Cards</strong> switches to the cards described below. A list (the response sends several values) has its options in the editor &#x2014; <strong>Options</strong> on its row opens it: <strong>Every value, as recorded</strong> or <strong>One at random each iteration</strong> (chosen before or after it is in the script), and for a list a later request sends back <strong>rebuilt</strong>, its fields, which one the items follow (<strong>One item for each value of</strong>) and <strong>Fill the missing paths</strong>.</p>
        <p class="help-p"><strong>Review</strong> on a row (or a click on it) opens the <strong>editor</strong>, one value at a time (&#x25B2;&#x25BC; for the previous / next): why it was found, <strong>Read from the response of</strong> (requests that have the value are marked), <strong>How to read it</strong> &#x2014; <strong>Auto</strong> (what the Correlation Advisor chose), <strong>JSON path</strong>, <strong>Boundaries</strong>, <strong>Regex</strong> (a regular expression: the first <code>( )</code> group is the value; keep to plain syntax, VuGen and DevWeb read it with their own engines), <strong>XPath</strong> (an XML response: element names without a prefix match in any namespace, <code>/@name</code> reads an attribute) or <strong>Header</strong> &#x2014; tested as you type (it says what it reads, and whether that is the value sent back), the name, and the code the script gets. On the right: the response with the value marked &#x2014; select or double-click a value to read it from there &#x2014; and every place it is <strong>Sent back in</strong>. <strong>Accept &amp; next</strong> ticks it; for a value already in the script the button is <strong>Edit</strong>: change it, then <strong>Save to the script</strong> (<strong>Cancel</strong> puts it back as it was) — a click on an item of <strong>Correlation details</strong> opens it here too; <strong>Add n to the script</strong> in the table puts it in. A value no later request sends back cannot be accepted; a value already in the script is shown, not changed; <strong>Undo my changes</strong> goes back to the Advisor's choice. An empty response offers <strong>Paste the response</strong>.</p>
        <p class="help-p">The correlations Studio finds by itself (sign-in tokens, redirect values, PKCE) are in the table too, already in the script and marked <strong>Found by Studio</strong>. <strong>Correlation details</strong> has <strong>Edit</strong> and <strong>Remove</strong> on every correlation: Edit opens the editor with its response; Remove takes it out (Undo puts it back), and it stays out when the recording is read again. <strong>Test</strong> &#x2014; in the editor, and in the table for the ticked values (or the ones in the script) &#x2014; checks each against the recording and shows the result in a window (drag it by its title to move it; for a list, what is sent each iteration — one at random, the next one, or always the same): what it reads, whether every place it is sent back sent that value, and with a second recording whether it changes. It does not run the script: a real run needs VuGen or DevWeb. In the editor, drag the bar between the two panels to resize them (or use its arrow keys; double-click puts it back), or hide one to a rail.</p>
        <p class="help-p">More in step 2: <strong>Take out</strong> on a value in the script takes it out again (Undo puts it back; to change how a value in the script is read, take it out first). A token the server renews &#x2014; the same name and field, a new value each time &#x2014; is one row, <strong>Renewed</strong>; <strong>Show the n</strong> opens each renewal, and the tick, name and Skip apply to all of them. A value no response sends before the page sends it was <strong>Made in the browser</strong> (a hash, an id the page&#x2019;s own script made): there is nothing to read it from, so it cannot be ticked. When it is a UUID &#x2014; an <code>X-Request-Id</code>, a correlation id, in a header, the query string or the body &#x2014; <strong>Generate a UUID</strong> (on a row of them, <strong>Generate UUIDs</strong>) has the script make a new one instead: before the first request that sends each recorded value, so a value the recording renewed is renewed at the same places and one it kept is kept for the iteration; then add it as any other value. <strong>Don&#x2019;t generate</strong> puts it back. Any other value can be generated too: open it and choose <strong>Generate (UUID)</strong> under How to read it (a value that is not a UUID gets a warning; another choice, or Undo my changes, puts it back). <strong>Names</strong> chooses how new names are written: the field&#x2019;s own name (<code>sessionId</code>) or with a p in front (<code>pSessionId</code>), remembered; names you typed and values in the script keep theirs. <strong>Also suggest other repeated values</strong> adds what the Advisor leaves out (values under 10 characters, numbers, past the first 20) that a response sends and a later request sends back, as Medium and not ticked &#x2014; tick only the ones that change for each user; typed values (test data) are not suggested.</p>
        <p class="help-p"><strong>Cards</strong> (the Table / Cards switch in step 2, <strong>Correlate</strong>) shows the Correlation Advisor&#x2019;s findings the earlier way — each as a card showing confidence level, its <strong>Source</strong> (the request whose response holds it) and where it is <strong>Replaced in</strong>, as request numbers you can click to open that request; <strong>+n more</strong> lists every place. Review each card and click <strong>Accept</strong> to add it as a correlation (<strong>Accept all high</strong> takes every high-confidence card at once), then click <strong>Apply &amp; Regenerate</strong> to rebuild the script. Its buttons: <strong>Scan again</strong> (reads the recording again with the current filters), <strong>Paste &amp; Detect</strong>, <strong>Add Manual</strong> and <strong>Find a value</strong>; <strong>Show an example</strong> opens a request that sends a found value back, on its Dynamic values tab. Each card shows the value, <strong>Source</strong> and <strong>Replaced in</strong>, the variable name, and <strong>Accept</strong>, <strong>Skip</strong> and <strong>Show request</strong> (opens the request that sends it); a card applied to the script is outlined in green, <strong>In the script</strong>. The counts, <strong>Accept all high</strong> and <strong>Apply &amp; Regenerate</strong> stay at the bottom. When it finds nothing, the step stays with <strong>Paste &amp; Detect</strong> and <strong>+ Add Manual</strong>, so you can add a correlation yourself.</p>
        <p class="help-p">You can add your own correlations manually (click <strong>+ Add Manual</strong>) or use <strong>Paste &amp; Detect</strong> when you know the value but it is not visible in the HAR.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Dynamic values of a request</h3>
        <p class="help-p">Click any request and open its <strong>Dynamic values</strong> tab. Every field in that request that looks dynamic is listed:</p>
        <ul class="help-steps-list">
          <li><span class="help-light help-light-ok">● Traced to an earlier response</span> — value traced to a prior server response, with the request that produced it. Click <strong>Add</strong> to correlate it in one click.</li>
          <li><span class="help-light help-light-warn">● Looks dynamic, source not found</span> — looks dynamic (JWT, UUID, or token pattern) but its source was not found in the HAR. Use <strong>Paste &amp; Detect</strong> to create the extractor manually.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="help-p">It scans request headers, JSON body, form parameters, query string, <strong>and URL path segments</strong> — so a session ID embedded in a REST URL like <code>/api/users/286522</code> is detected just like any other field.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Find a value</h3>
        <p class="help-p"><strong>Find a value</strong> answers where any value comes from and every request that sends it &#x2014; the same answer as in the <strong>HAR Explorer</strong> (see its <em>The answers Find a value gives</em>). When the verdict is <strong>Correlate it</strong>, <strong>Add and apply</strong> adds it to the Correlation Advisor and rebuilds the script.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Paste &amp; Detect</h3>
        <p class="help-p">In step 2, <strong>Correlate</strong>, open <strong>Can&#x2019;t find a value?</strong> and choose <strong>Paste &amp; Detect</strong> (in Cards, its button is in the Advisor header). Paste the server response body and the exact value you want to extract, then click <strong>Detect &amp; Generate</strong>. The tool automatically picks the best extractor (JSONPath for JSON, Boundary for HTML/XML, RegExp for JWT / UUID / hex tokens) and shows radio buttons or occurrence dropdowns when values appear multiple times. A boundary is widened until it captures this value first (a page often has the same <code>content="</code> many times). <strong>Add Correlation</strong> adds a card to the Advisor and regenerates the script: the value is extracted from the recorded response that holds it, and every later request that sends it uses the correlation. A name like <code>visitor-hmac</code> becomes <code>visitor_hmac</code> (parameter names take letters, digits and _).</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Extractor types — how they are chosen</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Extractor</th>
              <th>When used</th>
              <th>DevWeb function</th>
              <th>VuGen C function</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">JSONPath</td><td>JSON response bodies</td><td><code>JsonPathExtractor</code></td><td><code>web_reg_save_param_json</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Boundary</td><td>HTML, XML, plain text</td><td><code>BoundaryExtractor</code></td><td><code>web_reg_save_param</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">RegExp</td><td>JWT, UUID, hex, token shapes</td><td><code>RegExpExtractor</code></td><td><code>web_reg_save_param_regexp</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Header</td><td>Response header value</td><td><code>BoundaryExtractor</code> (Headers scope)</td><td><code>web_reg_save_param</code> (Search=Headers)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Test data</h3>
        <p class="help-p">Step 3, <strong>Test data</strong>, lists every parameter of the script (user names, search terms, form values &#x2026;) with the recorded value. Rename or remove a parameter (removed: the recorded value stays in the script), add your own, choose how each Vuser takes a value (<strong>Next row</strong>, <strong>Random</strong> or <strong>Unique</strong>; <strong>Each run</strong> or <strong>Once</strong>) and which data file it is in. Type more rows, or download the values as an Excel file, fill many rows and upload it again. The ZIP holds the data files (VuGen <code>.prm</code> / <code>.dat</code>, DevWeb <code>parameters.yml</code> and its <code>.csv</code> files). Values that are correlations are not test data and cannot be added here. Only the last change can be undone.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Request Filters</h3>
        <p class="help-p">By default only <strong>Fetch/XHR</strong> and <strong>Document</strong> requests are included — CSS, JS, fonts, images and media are left out, which suits server / API load. Select their types for realistic page load, and always for <strong>JavaScript-based apps</strong>: files that JavaScript or CSS requests are never in the page HTML, so <code>downloadHtmlStaticResources</code> / <code>Mode=HTML</code> cannot download them on their own. Included files are added to the page step that loaded them (<code>EXTRARES</code> / <code>resources: [...]</code>), never as separate requests. Known analytics/ads domains are always left out. Every change rebuilds the script and the Correlation Advisor&#x2019;s findings.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Static assets — should I include them?</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>You want</th>
              <th>Do this</th>
              <th>On replay</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Server / API load</td><td>Leave <strong>JS</strong>, <strong>CSS</strong>, <strong>Img</strong> and <strong>Font</strong> off (default)</td><td>Only images and stylesheets named in the page HTML are downloaded. Script files, and files that JavaScript or CSS requests (fonts, lazy-loaded code, images on later screens, analytics), are not.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Realistic page load, or a <strong>JavaScript-based app</strong> (React, Angular, Vue&hellip;)</td><td>Turn <strong>JS</strong>, <strong>CSS</strong>, <strong>Img</strong> and <strong>Font</strong> on (and untick <strong>Analytics/Ads</strong> if wanted)</td><td>Images and stylesheets in the HTML still download automatically; script files and files loaded by JavaScript/CSS are listed on their page step. Nothing is downloaded twice.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="help-p">Why: <code>downloadHtmlStaticResources</code> (DevWeb) and <code>Mode=HTML</code> (VuGen) read the page&#x2019;s HTML text and download what it names &#x2014; they do not run the page&#x2019;s JavaScript. Keep both on in every script. Included files are never separate requests: they are added to the page step that loaded them (VuGen <code>EXTRARES</code>, DevWeb <code>resources: [...]</code>).</p>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-amber">
          <strong>Not sure which kind your app is?</strong> Generate once with static assets included and read the comment above each page step, e.g. <code>// Page resources: 15 images/stylesheets in the page HTML download automatically; 2 other (scripts, CSS/JS-loaded files) are listed</code>. If many are listed, keep them included. VuGen also needs the run-time setting <strong>Download non-HTML resources</strong> switched on (it is on by default).<br><br>
          <strong>Never scripted:</strong> failed or cancelled loads, repeats of the same file on a page (counted in the comment as &#x201C;failed or repeated&#x201D;) and <code>data:</code> / <code>blob:</code> URLs. Files the browser reused from its cache on a later page are still listed on that page, as VuGen records them. Analytics beacons sent with POST stay as their own request. Iframes named in the page HTML download automatically with the page. In a transaction with no page load (single-page-app screens that only call APIs), resources are listed on the API request in progress.
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Server names — running against another environment</h3>
        <p class="help-p">Every server the script calls gets one parameter, named by what it does: the application you recorded is <code>App_Server</code>; <code>api.</code>, <code>login.</code> / <code>auth.</code> and <code>cdn.</code> / <code>static.</code> servers are <code>Api_Server</code>, <code>Auth_Server</code> and <code>Static_Server</code>; well-known services by their purpose (<code>Recaptcha_Server</code>, <code>Google_Fonts</code>, <code>Okta_Server</code> &hellip;); any other server <code>&lt;Organisation&gt;_Server</code> (e.g. <code>Partner_Server</code>). It is set once per Vuser &#x2014; VuGen: <code>set_server(&quot;App_Server&quot;, &quot;shop.example.com&quot;)</code> in <code>vuser_init.c</code>; DevWeb: a <code>const</code> at the top of <code>main.js</code> &#x2014; and used in every URL, header, page resource, form value and body that carries that server, including encoded forms such as <code>redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2F&hellip;</code>. To run against another environment, change the values in that one place. Only the servers the script really calls are listed. Script Studio and the Postman / Bruno and JMeter converters all name servers this way.</p>
        <p class="help-p"><strong>Without editing the script (VuGen):</strong> add an attribute with the parameter&#x2019;s name in <strong>Runtime Settings &#x203A; Additional attributes</strong> (e.g. <code>App_Server</code> = <code>staging.example.com</code>) &#x2014; it wins over the value in <code>vuser_init.c</code>, so one script runs against each environment from its test. <strong>DevWeb</strong> works the same way with a user argument of that name (<code>userArguments</code> in <code>rts.yml</code>, or <code>-userArgs</code>): <code>const App_Server = load.config.user.args[&quot;App_Server&quot;] || &#x27;shop.example.com&#x27;;</code></p>
        <p class="help-p"><strong>Rename:</strong> the <strong>Domains</strong> panel lists every server with its parameter &#x2014; click <strong>Rename</strong> (with Undo). <strong>Parameterization details</strong> in step 2, Correlate, lists them too, with the same Rename.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">How the generated VuGen script is laid out</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>vuser_init</strong> &#x2014; set once per Vuser: the servers, the SSL setting (<code>web_set_sockets_option</code>) and the headers <em>every</em> request sends with one value (<code>web_add_auto_header</code>). They stay set when <strong>Simulate a new user on each iteration</strong> is on. In a script made from a recording, the browser&#x2019;s User-Agent goes to the run-time settings (Browser emulation) instead.</li>
          <li><strong>Action</strong> &#x2014; the steps. A header that only some requests send stays on those requests. <code>Authorization</code>, cookies, DPoP proofs, <code>Origin</code> and CSRF tokens are never auto headers: they belong to one user or one request, and an auto header would also be sent on the sign-in request, before the token exists. <code>web_set_user</code> stays in Action.</li>
          <li><strong>Think time</strong> between steps, none after the last one (the iteration pacing follows). The run-time settings decide whether it is used.</li>
          <li><strong>Mode=HTML</strong> only on page loads (the response HTML is read for its images and stylesheets); API calls use <strong>Mode=HTTP</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>DPoP</strong> &#x2014; one fresh proof right before each request that sends one, for that request&#x2019;s own URL and token. <code>lre-utils.js</code> is loaded again at the top of Action, because VuGen empties its JavaScript engine on every new-user iteration.</li>
        </ul>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>Main application</strong> &#x2014; the server of the first page of your journey (marked <em>main</em> in the Domains list), not the server with the most requests, which is often an ad or analytics server.</li>
          <li><strong>Rename</strong> &#x2014; click <strong>Rename</strong> under a domain to give its parameter a meaningful name (e.g. <code>Shop_Server</code>). The script uses the new name everywhere, in both formats, also after a format change. Names already used by the script (<code>username</code>, <code>load</code>, &hellip;) are refused.</li>
          <li><strong>Only the server name is a parameter</strong>, not <code>https://</code>: the same server also appears without a scheme (headers, JSON) and URL-encoded (sign-in redirects), and a host-only value covers all of them.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 6b. HAR Explorer -->
  <div id="hacc-explorer" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-explore)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-explorer')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-explore)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="7"/><path d="m20 20-3.5-3.5"/><path d="M8 11h6M11 8v6"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">HAR Explorer</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Find where every dynamic value comes from · works where DevTools is switched off</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-purple"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="7"/><path d="m20 20-3.5-3.5"/><path d="M8 11h6M11 8v6"/></svg>HAR Explorer</div>
        <p class="help-p">Most failed replays come from values that change for every user &#x2014; session ids, tokens, CSRF fields, order numbers. When the Correlation Advisor misses one, or a replay fails on one, you need to know where the value comes from and where it goes. The HAR Explorer shows every request of a recording &#x2014; like the browser&#x2019;s DevTools, but grouped in the script&#x2019;s transactions and with no DevTools needed &#x2014; and answers that question for any value. Then it builds the correlation for Script Studio. The file is read in your browser; nothing is uploaded.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">When to use it</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>A replay fails on a value</strong> &#x2014; see the response it first comes from and every request that sends it back.</li>
          <li><strong>The Correlation Advisor did not list a value</strong> &#x2014; find it here and correlate it in one click.</li>
          <li><strong>A value never comes back from the server</strong> &#x2014; it was typed (a parameter) or made by the page (generate it in the script).</li>
          <li><strong>A HAR file from a client or a colleague</strong> &#x2014; look inside it on a machine where DevTools is switched off.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Step by step</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>Open <strong>HAR Explorer</strong> in the sidebar and drop the <code>.har</code> file on the page (or click <strong>Choose HAR file</strong>).</li>
          <li>The requests appear in their transactions, exactly as <strong>Script Studio</strong> groups them &#x2014; from the START / END bookmarks, the PerfX CDP Recorder or the page loads. Requests the script leaves out (images, analytics &#x2026;) are folded into one line per transaction; <strong>Show</strong> brings them back, and the eye button in the Requests header shows them all.</li>
          <li>Narrow the list: the request types above it (<strong>All</strong> / <strong>None</strong>; <strong>Filters</strong> folds the bar to one line), the address box at the top of the list, and the <strong>Domains</strong> panel on the left (every server with its number of requests &#x2014; switch a server off or on, <strong>Only</strong> to show just that one, <strong>All on</strong> / <strong>All off</strong>, and a box to find a server; on a smaller screen it is the <strong>Domains</strong> button). Domains, Requests and the details each fold to a slim bar with the button in their header, and come back with one click.</li>
          <li>Click a request to see its <strong>Headers</strong>, <strong>Payload</strong> (query, form fields, JSON body), <strong>Response</strong>, <strong>Preview</strong>, <strong>Cookies</strong>, <strong>Timing</strong> and <strong>Dynamic values</strong> (the values of this request that change, from Script Studio&#x2019;s own engine &#x2014; click one to find where it comes from). <strong>Hide secrets</strong> in the details header masks passwords, tokens and cookies for screenshots.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Find a value</strong> &#x2014; it opens over the page; its close button, Esc or a click beside it closes it. Type or paste the value, pick a suggestion (values the server sends that a later request sends back; <em>Studio</em> marks the ones Script Studio also finds by itself), or select the value in any request and click <strong>Find where this value comes from</strong>.</li>
          <li>Read the answer: the verdict, then the trail &#x2014; <strong>Source</strong> (the response and the exact place) and every place it is <strong>Replaced in</strong> (<strong>Sent in</strong> when it has no source in the recording). Click any of them to open that request with the value marked.</li>
          <li>When the verdict is <strong>Correlate it</strong>, <strong>Correlate this</strong> shows the extractor for <strong>VuGen Web HTTP/HTML</strong> or <strong>DevWeb</strong> &#x2014; the same code Script Studio writes. Check the parameter name and click <strong>Add to Script Studio</strong>. Repeat for other values.</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Continue in Script Studio</strong> (top right). Studio opens the same file, analyses it and applies your correlations, marked <em>From HAR Explorer</em>. With nothing added the button says <strong>Open in Script Studio</strong>: Studio only opens the file, with the script ready.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="help-h3">The answers Find a value gives</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Verdict</th>
              <th>What it means</th>
              <th>What to do</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Correlate it</td><td>The server sends it in an earlier response and the journey sends it back &#x2014; every virtual user gets a different one.</td><td><strong>Correlate this</strong> &#x2192; <strong>Add to Script Studio</strong>.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Nothing to do</td><td>It travels in cookies only, it is a redirect the script follows, or only requests left out of the script send it back.</td><td>Nothing &#x2014; VuGen and DevWeb handle cookies and redirects by themselves.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">A value typed in the journey</td><td>It never comes from the server: the user typed or chose it.</td><td>Make it a parameter with test data.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Made by the browser</td><td>It looks like a time stamp or a GUID made by the page&#x2019;s JavaScript.</td><td>Generate a new one in the script (VuGen <code>lr_save_timestamp</code> / <code>lr_generate_uuid</code>; DevWeb <code>Date.now()</code> / a uuid).</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Its source is not in this recording</td><td>Earlier responses were saved without their content (the browser served them from its cache), so the response it comes from is missing.</td><td>Record again with DevTools <em>Disable cache</em> ticked, or with the PerfX CDP Recorder, and look it up again.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-blue">
          <strong>Encoded forms are found too.</strong> A value sent URL-encoded, JSON-escaped, Base64 or as HTML entities is matched to the original and named in the trail. The <code>Referer</code> / <code>Origin</code> copy of a page address and requests left out of the script are listed apart and not counted.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 7a. Workload Modeller -->
  <div id="hacc-workload" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-work)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-workload')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-work)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 19V5M4 19h16"/><path d="m7 15 4-4 3 3 5-6"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Workload Modeller</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Targets per script → Vusers, pacing, ramp-up, load generators · LRE settings and Excel</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-purple"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 19V5M4 19h16"/><path d="m7 15 4-4 3 3 5-6"/></svg>Workload Modeller</div>
        <p class="help-p">Designs an LRE scenario from what each script must reach. You give each script a <strong>target</strong> — transactions per second (TPS), transactions per hour, a number of Vusers, a share of the total TPS or a number of iterations — and the modeller works out the <strong>Vusers</strong>, <strong>pacing</strong>, <strong>ramp-up and ramp-down</strong>, the <strong>load generators</strong> and the iterations, and warns when something cannot work. Everything is worked out in your browser — <strong>no file is uploaded or stored on the server</strong>.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">How to use it</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li><strong>The test</strong>: choose the kind of test (load, soak, stress, capacity, spike) and how long full load lasts.</li>
          <li><strong>Scripts</strong>: drop your script ZIPs (Web HTTP/HTML or DevWeb) or HAR recordings — each script's transactions, think time, fixed waits and requests are filled in. Fixed waits (<code>sleep</code>, DevWeb <code>load.sleep</code>) are not think time: LRE always waits them in full, whatever its Think Time settings say; <code>lr_think_time</code> / <code>load.thinkTime</code> follow those settings, including <strong>Limit think time to</strong>. Transactions in <code>vuser_init</code> / <code>vuser_end</code> (DevWeb: <code>load.initialize</code> / <code>load.finalize</code>) run once per Vuser and are not counted.</li>
          <li>To compare with an LRE test that already exists, set <strong>Ramp-up and ramp-down</strong> to <strong>My own, as in LRE</strong> and type its Start Vusers / Stop Vusers (for example 1 Vuser every 5 s); otherwise the tool chooses the ramp.</li>
          <li>Give each script its <strong>target</strong> and a <strong>response time</strong> (your SLA will do). Many scripts: switch to <strong>Table</strong>, paste a column from Excel, use <strong>Paste from Excel</strong> (script name + target), or use <strong>Fill in with Excel</strong> and drop the sheet back.</li>
          <li><strong>Load generators</strong>: choose the machine type — the on-premise Windows servers or AWS c6a.4xlarge (Linux or Windows); <strong>Another machine</strong> is for a project with its own load generators. Each machine type has the team rule — Vusers per machine for a script of web pages and for one of API calls (on-premise 100 / 250, AWS twice that); the tool reads each script's pages and API calls, and you can set <strong>Kind of script</strong> on its card. Vusers per machine is the smallest of the team rule, the machine's CPU and its memory. <strong>Extra machines</strong> carry Vusers too — the scripts are spread over all of them. <strong>Fill each machine</strong> (the default) fills every machine; a script that does not fit whole runs as two groups in LRE: LRE setup shows the script on two rows, each with its load generators and schedule — add the script once per row. <strong>One group per script</strong> is simpler to set up but may leave machines half full. Better still, enter how many Vusers one machine carried in a past run. The machine types and their figures come from the team settings file on the server (<code>workload-modeller/config/workload-settings.json</code>) — the line under the pools says which figures are in use.</li>
          <li>The notes at the top of <strong>Scripts</strong> say what each box means. <strong>Think time</strong> is the total of one iteration (10 transactions with 3 s between them = 27 s), not per transaction; <strong>Response time</strong> is per transaction.</li>
          <li>Read the <strong>Result</strong> and the <strong>Checks</strong>. The tiles give <strong>Transactions per second (TPS)</strong>, <strong>Transactions per hour (TPH)</strong> and <strong>Iterations per hour</strong> at full load, how long <strong>one iteration</strong> takes (where think time shows — with a fixed pacing it changes the TPS only when the iteration no longer fits), then the <strong>Total test duration</strong> and the totals of the whole test (ramp-up and ramp-down included). <strong>How to read these numbers</strong>, under the tiles, works them out with your own figures. <strong>Details › LRE setup</strong> has one row per script with everything to build the scenario in LRE — Vusers, load generators (LG1, LG2 …), pacing, think time and LRE's own fields Start (ramp-up), Duration (steady state) and Stop (ramp-down), in HH:MM:SS. With a schedule by group every group starts immediately and all reach full load together; <strong>Load generators</strong> shows what each machine runs. Then use <strong>Copy LRE settings</strong> or <strong>Download Excel</strong> at the top of the page — they stay in view while you scroll.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Good to know</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>Defaults for every script</strong> stay in force: they fill only empty fields, also in scripts added later. <strong>Overwrite all scripts</strong> replaces each script's own value once (you can undo it).</li>
          <li>The rule behind every number: TPS = Vusers × transactions per iteration ÷ pacing, and the pacing must be at least one iteration (response times + think time). When it is not, the script card says <strong>Not possible</strong> with the reason in figures and what to change (a longer pacing, more Vusers to keep the TPS, or a shorter iteration) — the first two with a button, which Undo takes back. <strong>How it is worked out</strong> shows each step for each script, and <strong>All the formulas</strong> lists every rule the tool uses.</li>
          <li>The growth factor raises TPS targets; scripts sized by Vusers or iterations keep their numbers.</li>
          <li>Machine figures from CPU and memory are starting assumptions — check the load generators' CPU and memory in the first run and correct the figure.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 7b. Results Analyser -->
  <div id="hacc-analyser" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-results)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-analyser')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-results)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 3v18h18"/><path d="M7 15v-4M11 15V8M15 15v-6M19 15V5"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Results Analyser</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">LRE raw results → transaction summary, steady state, insights · Excel / Word / PDF</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-tool-badge help-badge-purple"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 3v18h18"/><path d="M7 15v-4M11 15V8M15 15v-6M19 15V5"/></svg>Results Analyser</div>
        <p class="help-p">Analyses a LoadRunner Enterprise run from its <strong>raw results</strong>, independently of LRE Analysis. The files are read by your browser on your PC — <strong>nothing is uploaded or stored on the server</strong>, whatever the size of the run.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">How to use it</h3>
        <ol class="help-steps-list">
          <li>In LRE, download the run's <strong>raw results</strong> (the ZIP that contains <code>RawData.eve</code>, <code>_t_rep.eve</code> and <code>res*.lrr</code>).</li>
          <li>Open <strong>Results Analyser</strong> in the sidebar and choose the ZIP — or, faster for very large runs, unzip it and choose the folder.</li>
          <li>Review the tabs: <strong>Overview</strong>, <strong>Transactions</strong> (full run and steady state), <strong>Charts</strong>, <strong>Errors</strong>, <strong>Workload</strong> and <strong>Insights</strong>.</li>
          <li>In <strong>Workload</strong>, click a load generator to see its scripts, the VUsers that stopped early on it and its CPU and memory over time (the Controller runs no VUsers, so it shows N/A). <em>How to read it</em> explains how <strong>Iterations planned</strong> is worked out from the pacing and what <strong>Stopped early</strong> counts.</li>
          <li>Optional: change the <strong>analysis window</strong> (default = detected steady state) and enter <strong>SLA</strong> thresholds — all figures and insights update.</li>
          <li>Export <strong>Excel</strong> (7 sheets, including Charts), <strong>Word</strong> (editable report), <strong>HTML</strong> (a web page you can keep or email) or <strong>PDF</strong> (print dialog → Save as PDF). The reports carry the figures, tables and charts; the insights stay on the page (Overview and Insights tabs).</li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="help-h3">How the numbers are computed</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>Response times</strong> come from every transaction sample in <code>RawData.eve</code>. Percentiles are exact (nearest-rank) for runs up to 20 million samples, and within 0.5 % above that.</li>
          <li><strong>Steady state</strong> = from the moment the last VUser starts running to the moment the first VUser exits (from <code>_t_rep.eve</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Pass / fail</strong> counts come from LRE's own sum_data graphs, used only when they reproduce the RawData.eve counts.</li>
          <li><strong>Insights</strong> are offline statistical checks: pacing and Little's Law, trend (Mann-Kendall), change points, spikes (median/MAD), load-generator comparison, LG CPU and memory, errors, SLA and Apdex.</li>
          <li>The technical self-checks are in the result data export (<strong>More</strong> → Result data (JSON)) and the command-line output. Transaction counts should match LRE Analysis exactly; percentiles may differ slightly because LRE interpolates.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Very large runs</h3>
        <p class="help-p">Any size works in the browser because files are streamed. For runs of hundreds of GB, the same engine runs from the command line on a machine that has the files: <code>node lre-results-analyser/cli/analyse.js "D:\Results\Run_59" --xlsx run59.xlsx --html run59.html</code>. Add <code>--redact</code> to remove host names and IP addresses before sharing the output.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="help-group-h">Capabilities</h2>

  <!-- 3. Output Formats -->
  <div id="hacc-formats" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-formats')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h10a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><path d="M14 3v5h5"/><path d="m10 13-2 2 2 2M14 13l2 2-2 2"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Output Formats</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">DevWeb JavaScript vs Web HTTP/HTML C — which to choose?</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <p class="help-p">All three script tools — the two converters and Script Studio — can generate scripts in either of two formats. The HAR Explorer writes its correlation code in either format too.</p>
        <div class="help-formats">
          <div class="help-format-card help-format-blue">
            <div class="help-format-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h10a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><path d="M14 3v5h5"/><path d="m10 13-2 2 2 2M14 13l2 2-2 2"/></svg>DevWeb · JavaScript</div>
            <p class="help-format-desc">The modern LoadRunner protocol. Scripts are written in JavaScript and run in a Node.js-like environment. Generates a <code>main.js</code> file.</p>
            <p class="help-format-rec"><strong>Choose this if:</strong> your team uses LRE 2021 or later, or you are comfortable with JavaScript.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="help-format-card help-format-orange">
            <div class="help-format-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14 3H7a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v14a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h10a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><path d="M14 3v5h5"/><path d="m10 13-2 2 2 2M14 13l2 2-2 2"/></svg>Web HTTP/HTML · C</div>
            <p class="help-format-desc">The classic VuGen protocol. Scripts are written in C and use familiar VuGen functions like <code>web_url()</code> and <code>web_custom_request()</code>. Generates an <code>Action.c</code> file.</p>
            <p class="help-format-rec"><strong>Choose this if:</strong> your team uses older LRE versions or prefers the traditional VuGen scripting style.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="help-p">Not sure? Ask your LRE administrator which protocol your project uses. When in doubt, choose <strong>DevWeb</strong> — it is the current default for new projects.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 8. Capabilities -->
  <div id="hacc-capabilities" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-capabilities')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 21v-7M4 10V3M12 21v-9M12 8V3M20 21v-5M20 12V3M1 14h6M9 8h6M17 16h6"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Capabilities</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Auth methods, correlation, parameterisation — all handled automatically</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <p class="help-p">The toolkit detects and generates code for the following — no manual configuration needed.</p>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Authentication</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Method</th>
              <th>What the toolkit does</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">OAuth2</td><td>Client Credentials, Password Grant, Authorization Code flows</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">JWT</td><td>Detects signing libraries (jsrsasign, jose, JJWT, nimbus), extracts claim mappings, generates token creation code</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">DPoP</td><td>RFC 9449 – generates unique cryptographic proofs per request using EC P-256 keys. Detected from <code>dpop</code> / <code>dpop-pf</code> headers.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">PKCE</td><td>RFC 7636 – generates <code>code_verifier</code> + <code>code_challenge</code> per iteration. Detected from <code>code_challenge</code> in auth URLs and <code>code_verifier</code> in token exchange bodies.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Kerberos / NTLM</td><td>Windows SSO – generates <code>web_set_user()</code> in VuGen, <code>setUserCredentials()</code> in DevWeb, configures runtime settings</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Basic / Bearer / API Key</td><td>Username/password, static or correlated tokens, header-based or query parameter keys</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">AWS Signature v4</td><td>AWS API request signing</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">mTLS</td><td>Client certificate authentication – upload <code>.pem</code> or <code>.p12</code> files alongside your collection</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Analysis &amp; optimisation</h3>
        <ul class="help-list">
          <li><strong>Correlation</strong> – finds dynamic values (tokens, session IDs, CSRF tokens) in responses and extracts them for reuse in later requests</li>
          <li><strong>3-tier parameterisation</strong> – classifies variables as Dynamic (runtime), Config (environment), or Test Data (per-user)</li>
          <li><strong>Server host detection</strong> – parameterises hostnames so you can switch environments (dev/test/prod) by changing one value — each server named by its role (<code>App_Server</code>, <code>Api_Server</code> …), see <em>Script Studio → Server names</em></li>
          <li><strong>Page resources</strong> – images, stylesheets and scripts are added to the page step that loaded them, as VuGen's own HTML-mode recording does</li>
          <li><strong>Noise left out</strong> – analytics and ad servers, browser reports and prefetches are left out of the script, with the reason shown</li>
          <li><strong>Redirect detection</strong> – identifies HTTP 301/302/307 redirects that VuGen follows automatically and omits them from the script</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Accepted input formats</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Format</th>
              <th>Source</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Postman Collection v2.1</td><td><code>.json</code> – Postman → Export → Collection v2.1</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Bruno Collection</td><td><code>.json</code> / <code>.yml</code> / <code>.bru</code> / folder (zipped)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">JMeter Test Plan</td><td><code>.jmx</code> – with optional CSV data files and certificates</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">HAR file</td><td><code>.har</code> – recorded with Start recording (CDP Recorder), or any browser → F12 → Network → Export HAR</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">LRE raw results</td><td>The run's raw results ZIP or unzipped folder (<code>RawData.eve</code>, <code>_t_rep.eve</code>, <code>res*.lrr</code>) – Results Analyser</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="help-group-h">Reference</h2>

  <!-- 9. Privacy & Security -->
  <div id="hacc-privacy" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-privacy')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Privacy &amp; Security</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">Files deleted when the conversion ends · scripts in memory only · no data retained</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <div class="help-callout help-callout-green">
          <strong><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg>Designed for regulated environments.</strong> Files uploaded to the converters are kept only for the length of the conversion: in memory, and in the server&#x2019;s temporary folder because the parsers read from a file. They are deleted when the conversion ends, whether it succeeds or fails. Generated scripts are never written to disk, and no data persists between requests. This design was chosen specifically to suit enterprise and regulated environments where data handling policies are strict.
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Data handling — what happens to your files</h3>
        <div class="help-table-wrap">
          <table class="help-table">
            <thead><tr>
              <th>Question</th>
              <th>Answer</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Which tools upload my file?</td><td>Only the <strong>Postman / Bruno</strong> and <strong>JMeter</strong> converters — the rows below describe what happens to those uploads. <strong>Script Studio</strong>, the <strong>HAR Explorer</strong> and the <strong>Results Analyser</strong> read your file in your browser; recordings and results never leave your PC.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">What does the CDP Recorder send?</td><td>Nothing to the internet. It runs on your PC, talks only to your own browser and to this page through <code>127.0.0.1</code>, and saves recordings to your Downloads folder (or next to <code>cdp-recorder.exe</code>). Only pages you allowed can start a recording. Its setup is one <code>perfx-recorder:</code> link in the Windows registry for your user and, for pages you allowed, <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\PerfX\cdp-recorder\allowed-pages.json</code>; each recording uses a temporary browser profile, deleted when it closes.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Is my usage recorded?</td><td>Each tool reports simple usage figures for the statistics — which tool, when, the file name and size, the number of requests, success or error, and the machine name. Never the content of a file, recording, script or result.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Is my file saved to disk?</td><td><strong>Only during the conversion.</strong> Files are received into memory (RAM). Collection, environment, JMX, CSV and certificate files are also written to the server&#x2019;s temporary folder while the conversion runs, because the parsers read from a file, and deleted when it ends, whether it succeeds or fails. Generated scripts are never written to disk.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Is my data stored between requests?</td><td><strong class="help-yes">No.</strong> Your uploaded files are deleted when the conversion ends. The generated scripts wait in memory for your download, until you download them or the 5-minute link expires, and are then released.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Can anyone else see my upload?</td><td><strong class="help-yes">No.</strong> Nothing in the portal lets another user reach your files or scripts, and a download link works once. Server logs do not contain file contents.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Are external services called?</td><td><strong class="help-yes">No.</strong> All processing is local. No data leaves the server other than the ZIP file returned to you.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">How long does the download link last?</td><td>Download tokens expire after <strong>5 minutes</strong> and are single-use. Once clicked, the token is invalidated. Run the conversion again if it expires — it takes only a few seconds.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="help-td-key">Do HAR files contain sensitive data?</td><td>Yes — HAR files contain all request headers, cookies, and response bodies from your recorded session. This may include session tokens, API keys, or personal data. Treat your HAR files as sensitive, use them only on trusted machines, and delete them after conversion.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3 class="help-h3">Technical stack</h3>
        <div class="help-cards">
          <div class="help-card" style="--c-tool:var(--c-convert)">
            <div class="help-card-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="7" rx="2"/><rect x="3" y="13" width="18" height="7" rx="2"/><path d="M7 7.5h.01M7 16.5h.01"/></svg>Server</div>
            <p class="help-card-p"><strong>Node.js</strong> + <strong>Express</strong>. File uploads handled by <strong>Multer</strong> with in-memory storage; the parsers read a temporary copy, deleted when the conversion ends. File size limits enforced at the upload boundary. Served on your internal network only.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="help-card" style="--c-tool:var(--c-studio)">
            <div class="help-card-title"><svg class="help-ico" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg>Crypto library</div>
            <p class="help-card-p"><code>lre-utils.dat</code> — zero-dependency crypto runtime for VuGen scripts. Handles EC P-256 key gen, ECDSA signing, RSA-PSS (PS256), SHA-256, PKCE pair generation. DevWeb uses Node.js built-in <code>crypto</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 10. Glossary -->
  <div id="hacc-glossary" class="help-acc-item open" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)">
    <button class="help-acc-hdr" onclick="haccToggle('hacc-glossary')">
      <div class="help-acc-hdr-left">
        <div class="help-acc-icon" style="--c-tool:var(--c-help)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20V3H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 0 4 5.5z"/><path d="M4 19.5V21h16"/></svg></div>
        <div>
          <div class="help-acc-title">Glossary</div>
          <div class="help-acc-sub">VuGen, LRE, HAR, correlation, JWT, DPoP, PKCE and more</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <svg class="help-acc-chevron" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="5 8 10 13 15 8"/></svg>
    </button>
    <div class="help-acc-body">
      <div class="help-acc-inner">
        <dl class="help-glossary">
          <dt>VuGen</dt>
          <dd>Virtual User Generator — the tool in LoadRunner Enterprise used to create and edit performance test scripts that simulate real users.</dd>
          <dt>LRE / LoadRunner Enterprise</dt>
          <dd>The performance testing platform. Scripts created by this toolkit are designed to run on LRE.</dd>
          <dt>DevWeb</dt>
          <dd>A modern LoadRunner protocol where scripts are written in JavaScript. Scripts run as <code>main.js</code>.</dd>
          <dt>Web HTTP/HTML (C)</dt>
          <dd>The classic LoadRunner protocol where scripts are written in C. Scripts run as <code>Action.c</code>.</dd>
          <dt>HAR file</dt>
          <dd>HTTP Archive — a JSON file exported from your browser's Developer Tools that contains a complete recording of all network requests made during a session.</dd>
          <dt>Correlation</dt>
          <dd>The process of capturing a dynamic value (like a token or session ID) from a server response and automatically inserting it into later requests, so the script works correctly on every run.</dd>
          <dt>Parameterization</dt>
          <dd>Replacing a hard-coded value (like a username or password) in a script with a variable that reads from a data file, so different virtual users use different data.</dd>
          <dt>Transaction</dt>
          <dd>A named measurement boundary in a VuGen script. LoadRunner records response time for each transaction separately, so you can see performance for specific user actions (e.g. "Login", "Search", "Checkout").</dd>
          <dt>OAuth2</dt>
          <dd>An industry-standard authorization framework. Applications use it to obtain access tokens from an authorization server, which are then sent with each API request.</dd>
          <dt>JWT (JSON Web Token)</dt>
          <dd>A compact, signed token used for authentication. Contains claims (like client ID, audience, expiry) and is signed with a private key. The toolkit generates JWT creation code automatically when detected.</dd>
          <dt>DPoP (Demonstrating Proof-of-Possession)</dt>
          <dd>A security mechanism (RFC 9449) that binds an access token to a specific cryptographic key. Each request includes a unique signed proof – preventing stolen tokens from being reused by attackers.</dd>
          <dt>PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange)</dt>
          <dd>A security extension to the OAuth2 Authorization Code flow (RFC 7636). The client generates a random <code>code_verifier</code> and sends a derived <code>code_challenge</code> in the authorization request. Prevents authorization code interception attacks. Generated fresh each iteration — not correlated from a server response.</dd>
          <dt>mTLS (Mutual TLS)</dt>
          <dd>Two-way certificate authentication where both the client and server present certificates. Upload your client certificate (<code>.pem</code> or <code>.p12</code>) alongside your collection.</dd>
          <dt>CSRF token</dt>
          <dd>Cross-Site Request Forgery token – a server-generated value included in forms and headers to prevent unauthorized requests. The toolkit extracts and correlates these automatically.</dd>
          <dt>CDP Recorder</dt>
          <dd>A small Windows program from PerfX Studio that records your journey in Edge or Chrome through the browser's own debugging interface (Chrome DevTools Protocol) — new tabs and pop-ups included, no browser extension and no administrator rights needed. It saves the recording as a HAR file.</dd>
          <dt>Server parameter</dt>
          <dd>One parameter per server the script calls (for example <code>App_Server</code>), set once per virtual user. Change it in one place — or in the run-time settings — to run the same script against another environment.</dd>
          <dt>Steady state</dt>
          <dd>The part of a test run when all virtual users are running: from the moment the last one starts to the moment the first one stops. The Results Analyser reports it separately from the full run.</dd>
          <dt>Pacing / think time</dt>
          <dd>Pacing sets how often a virtual user starts a new iteration; think time is the pause between steps inside an iteration, like a real user reading a page.</dd>
          <dt>Raw results (LRE)</dt>
          <dd>The files LoadRunner Enterprise keeps for a run (<code>RawData.eve</code>, <code>_t_rep.eve</code>, <code>res*.lrr</code>) — downloaded as a ZIP and read by the Results Analyser.</dd>
          <dt>CSVDataSet (JMeter)</dt>
          <dd>A JMeter feature that reads test data from a CSV file at runtime. When converting a JMX file, upload any CSV files your script references alongside the .jmx.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

